<article class="env-lemma" id="00GZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00GZ">Lemma <span data-tag="00GZ">10.37.6</span>.</a> A principal ideal domain is normal. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $R$ be a principal ideal domain. Let $g = a/b$ be an element of the fraction field of $R$ integral over $R$. Because $R$ is a principal ideal domain we may divide out a common factor of $a$ and $b$ and assume $(a, b) = R$. In this case, any equation $(a/b)^ n + r_{n-1} (a/b)^{n-1} + \ldots + r_0 = 0$ with $r_ i \in R$ would imply $a^ n \in (b)$. This contradicts $(a, b) = R$ unless $b$ is a unit in $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>